After having left innumerable Valkyries, Barbarians, Samurai, Knights, and the
odd Archeologist and Priest as carrion at Ludios, the Castle, the Quest, and
Gehennom, Gehennom, Gehennom, I have finally managed to ascend a Tourist.

Relevant details:  Level 30, AC -55, GDSM, GoP, Silver  Shield, Jumping boots,
Dwarvish Helm, Hawaiian Shirt, 3 Amulet of life saving, Vorpal Sword, and 
Frost Brand, and most importantly, the PYEC.  5.16 million points.

The first half of the game was made possible by my identifying a wand that did
nothing by zapping my dog.  Suddenly, I was the proud owner of an Ice Devil
named Fifi.  That, plus the Frost Brand which appeared with sacrificing on 
the Town level, made sure that very few monsters got more than a  hit or two 
on me.  That was the up side, the down side was that my "dog" was responsible
for my being credited with the death of 5 guards, 4 shopkeepers, 2 coaligned 
priests, the Oracle, and Croesus.  However, the "dog"'s assistance on the 
quest was a great aid.  The second half went quickly due to the PYEC and
a collection of scrolls from a deserted shop (thanks to the "dog"), which 
enabled the creating of the absurdly low armour class (the money from Ludios
given to the one priest the 'dog' missed didn't hurt, either).  From there on
it was dig to create a shortest path from stairs to stairs and
ensure that I always had at least one wand of death charged.

Four cursed scrolls of gold detection later, and a lucky guess as to
which direction to go, and I was standing at the Altar of The Lady,
offered the amulet, and departed the land of the mortal.

A few comments: (please forgive those that may have been made before, these
are ones my coworkers and I have come up with over the last few months of 
playing.

  This seems to be a common complaint, but Hell is *boring*, with a couple of
exceptions (the ghost town, and the water level).  If one mixes various views
of Hell from a variety of sources, one could easily visualize a lava and fire
level, an ice level, etc.  You should also find other dead characters in one
form or another doing penance.  Allow for the possibility of one of the attacks
by the demon princes (who are presumably presiding over a group of devils and
damned souls) to be punishment, as in the ball and chain.  (you see here
3 heavy iron balls, all chained to you)

 Secondly, it would be nice to be able to converse more freely with neutral or
friendly monsters, thereby possibly learning more as you went along.  This 
would lead to the suggestion that certain items could not be wished for until
you knew about them from some source within the game.  Suggestions would 
include the various coloured dragon mail, artifacts, and probably certain
other enchanted items.  

A small suggestion would include that certain intensely useful items, 
such as the polished silver shield, be added to the list of things that 
could be granted you from sacrificing to your god (as was the original given
to Perseus).

Suppose if  the 'threaten' command from Omega were added, allowing one to 
shake down monsters if they feel intimidated by you.  You could also possibly
add an intrinsic, based upon level, AC, stats, and weaponry, as to what level
of monster you automatically intimidate (you are stealthy, you intimidate
5 HD monsters).  There would be certain monsters (Balrog, Archon) that would
never be intimidated, and others (gnomes) that would break and run when they
saw you.  This would presumably be alignment specific, with robbery by lawfuls
being frowned upon.

Finally, what if a forge was added, where you could bring in starting materials
and have a weapon or armour made from them, for a fee (possibly non-monetary,
as in another small quest).  Allow for the creation of a weapon similar to 
the Vorpal Blade, but tuned to a monster other than Jabberwock.  The player
would not get a choice, but rather the monster would be chosen at random,
modified by player luck, player level, how much beyond teh basic cost 
dictated by the forgemaster one is willing to pay, and the level of the 
intended target.  Pay very well, and one could own a one hit TrollSlayer.
Do the minimum, and end up with a weapon that does absolutely awesome damage
against kobolds.

Just suggestions and comments from the team.  However, it is an delightful game
in its present form, and my best bow to the Dev Team.

